        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Lists", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "New vs. Used Treadmill", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/new-vs-used-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it worth buying a used treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Under about $900, almost always. At that budget new buys a 40 to 55-inch deck and a light frame, while used buys a machine that originally cost two or three times as much, with a 60-inch deck and 250 lbs of frame. Above $2,000 the argument weakens, because new is already good hardware with full warranty cover.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much should a used treadmill cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Roughly half the original price at two years and about a third at three to five, then it flattens because the remaining value is essentially the frame. A machine that sold for $1,800 new should be around $600 at four years in average condition, less if it was never lubricated.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What should I check when buying a used treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Run it at several speeds and listen for a rumble that rises with speed. Stand on it and confirm the belt does not hesitate under load. Watch belt tracking for several minutes. Slide a hand under the belt — it should feel slightly slick, not dry or gritty. Check the deck for polishing, and test every console function including incline.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do treadmill warranties transfer to a second owner?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost never. Manufacturer warranties are typically non-transferable, so assume zero coverage on any private sale regardless of how much time is nominally remaining. Refurbished and dealer purchases are the exception, usually carrying 90 days to a year, often parts-only.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the most common problem with used treadmills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A motor and controller board worn by years of running against a dry, unlubricated deck. It is the expensive failure and it is nearly always caused by skipped maintenance rather than age. That is why the single most informative question to a seller is how often they lubricated it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a refurbished treadmill better than a used one?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most buyers, yes. Wear parts are typically replaced, a limited warranty applies and delivery is usually handled, which removes the three main risks of private sale. You pay more than private sale and much less than new, and the constraint is availability — you buy what exists rather than what you chose.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-08",
