          <a href="#when-new-wins" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. When New Is Clearly the Right Answer</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Final Recommendations</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">12. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A used treadmill buys roughly twice the hardware per dollar, and that is not a marginal
          advantage — it is the difference between a walking machine and a running one at the same
          budget. What it costs you is recourse, and whether that trade is sound depends almost
          entirely on how carefully you inspect.
        </p>

        <div id="verdict-up-front" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Verdict, Up Front</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Choose new if</strong> you want a warranty and someone to call, you need the
            machine delivered into the room rather than onto the driveway, you are not confident
            assessing a motor by ear, or you simply do not want to spend a weekend arranging
            collection. You are paying roughly double for hardware and getting certainty back.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose used if</strong> your budget is under about $900 and you want a running
            deck, you are comfortable inspecting mechanical equipment, and you have a way to move
            a 250 lb object. At that budget the used market is not marginally better — it is the
            only route to hardware that will not frustrate you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose refurbished if</strong> you want most of the used-market value with an
            actual warranty attached, and you can wait for the right machine to appear.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of this page is about how to tell a good used machine from a bad one, because
            that is where the entire risk sits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-money-buys" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What the Same Money Buys</h2>
<p>
            Treadmills depreciate steeply and then flatten, which is exactly the shape that
            rewards a secondhand buyer. A machine typically sells for roughly half its original
            price at two years and around a third at three to five, then holds close to that for
            years afterwards because the remaining value is essentially the frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            At <strong>$500</strong>: new buys a 40 to 50-inch deck, a 1.5 to 2.25 CHP motor and a
            frame under 130 lbs. Used buys a three-to-five-year-old mid-range machine with a 55 to
            60-inch deck, 2.5 to 3.0 CHP and 200 lbs or more of frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            At <strong>$800</strong>: new buys a 55-inch deck and about 190 lbs. Used buys what
            cost $1,800 to $2,500 new — a 60-inch deck, 3.0 to 3.5 CHP and 250 lbs.
          </p>
          <p>
            At <strong>$1,500</strong>: new buys a good mid-range machine with a full warranty.
            Used buys commercial-grade hardware. This is the point where the decision genuinely
            balances, because new at this price is already sufficient for most people.
          </p>
          <p>
            Above <strong>$2,000</strong>, new increasingly wins. You are buying warranty coverage
            on components whose replacement cost is significant, and the hardware advantage of
            going used narrows because you were already buying good hardware.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Actually Fails on a Used Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Knowing the failure hierarchy tells you what to inspect and what to ignore.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor and controller board.</strong> The expensive failures, and almost always
            caused by the same thing: belt-to-deck friction rising because nobody lubricated the
            machine. Replacement typically runs several hundred dollars with labour, which on a
            $500 purchase is most of the value.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck surface.</strong> Decks wear through their low-friction coating and
            eventually need flipping or replacing. A polished, shiny or grooved surface under the
            belt means wear is advanced and friction is already elevated.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt.</strong> A genuine wear item, and the cheapest of these to fix. A worn
            belt is not a reason to walk away, only to negotiate.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rollers and bearings.</strong> Rarely fail outright and announce themselves
            clearly when they do, as a rumble or grinding that rises with speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console electronics.</strong> Occasionally fail, often expensively on
            screen-based machines, and are the strongest argument for buying a used machine with a
            simple LED console rather than a touchscreen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inspection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Inspection That Matters</h2>
<p>
            Never buy a used treadmill you have not run. A seller unwilling to have it plugged in
            and working is telling you something, and the reason is almost never innocent.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Run it at several speeds</strong>, from slow walking to at least 6 mph, for a
            few minutes. Listen for a rumble that rises with speed, which suggests bearings, and
            for any change in pitch under load when you step on.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stand on it and watch the belt.</strong> It should not hesitate or slow when
            you step on. A belt that lags under load means friction is already high, which is the
            precursor to motor failure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check belt tracking.</strong> Watch for drift toward one rail over several
            minutes. Minor drift is adjustable and normal; a belt that walks steadily to one side
            may indicate a bent frame or worn roller.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Feel under the belt.</strong> Slide a hand between belt and deck near the
            middle. It should feel slightly slick. Dry, hot or gritty means it has not been
            lubricated, and you should assume the motor has already spent part of its life.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look at the deck surface</strong> for polishing or grooves, and
            <strong>test every console function</strong>, incline included — incline motors are a
            common and awkward failure. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">full fifteen-point checklist</a>
            covers the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ask-the-seller" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Four Questions Worth Asking</h2>
<p>
            <strong>How often was it lubricated?</strong> The single most informative question. An
            owner who answers specifically has maintained the machine. "You have to lubricate
            them?" means you should assume none was done and price accordingly — this is the
            answer you will hear most often.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How many hours or miles has it done?</strong> Many consoles track this. A
            machine with 200 hours has barely started; one with 3,000 is well through its life.
            If the owner does not know, the console usually does under a maintenance menu.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why are you selling it?</strong> "We stopped using it" is the common and
            reassuring answer, and it is why the best used machines are lightly used ones.
            "It started making a noise" is a different conversation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do you have the manual and the original account details?</strong> The manual
            matters because it tells you the deck type — pre-waxed decks must never be lubricated
            with silicone. On connected machines, account registration may need releasing before
            you can use the console fully, and that is far easier to sort out before money changes
            hands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Where to Buy Used, Ranked</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Manufacturer refurbished and open-box.</strong> The best risk-adjusted option.
            Wear parts are typically replaced, a limited warranty applies, and delivery is usually
            handled. You pay more than private sale and considerably less than new, and you buy
            what happens to be available rather than what you chose.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Specialist used fitness equipment dealers.</strong> Machines are serviced
            before resale and often carry a short warranty. Prices sit above private sale. These
            are the people who buy from gyms, so commercial-grade stock appears here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Gym and hotel closures.</strong> Commercial machines at low prices, sold in
            volume and usually as-is. Excellent value if you can assess condition and arrange heavy
            transport, and these machines are built for duty cycles a home will never approach.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Private sale.</strong> The lowest prices and the highest variance. This is
            where the "used twice, must go" machines are, and also where the neglected ones are.
            The inspection above is doing all the work here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moving-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Moving It Is Your Problem, and It Is a Real One</h2>
<p>
            The cost people forget is transport. A mid-range treadmill is 200 to 300 lbs, awkward
            to lift and impossible to angle around a tight turn. This is not a two-friends-and-a-car
            job.
          </p>
          <p>
            Plan it before agreeing a price. Measure the route at both ends, including doorway
            widths and the diagonal of any stairwell turn. Establish whether the machine can be
            partially disassembled — many fold at the upright, which sheds several inches — and
            ask the seller to have it folded and ready. Budget for a van and, realistically, two
            capable people.
          </p>
          <p>
            Factor $80 to $200 for a van hire or a courier into the comparison. It narrows the
            gap against new — where delivery is often included — but rarely closes it. What it
            does do is turn a $500 bargain into a $650 one, which changes the arithmetic against a
            refurbished machine with delivery included.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What You Give Up Legally</h2>
<p>
            Manufacturer warranties on treadmills are almost universally non-transferable, so a
            two-year-old machine with three years of parts cover remaining transfers none of it to
            you. Assume zero warranty on any private sale.
          </p>
          <p>
            Private sales are also sold as seen in most jurisdictions, with no returns and no
            recourse if a fault appears the following week. That is the fundamental trade of the
            used market and it is why the inspection matters so much — it is your only protection.
          </p>
          <p>
            Refurbished and dealer sales are different and this is most of what you pay extra for.
            Warranties are typically 90 days to a year, often parts-only, and narrower than new
            coverage — but they exist, and on a machine whose motor might cost several hundred
            dollars to replace, that is meaningful rather than symbolic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-week" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What to Do in the First Week</h2>
<p>
            Whatever you buy used, service it immediately rather than waiting for a problem. You
            are inheriting an unknown maintenance history and the cheapest moment to correct it is
            now.
          </p>
          <p>
            Lubricate the belt — after checking the manual for deck type, because some are
            pre-waxed and silicone ruins them. Check and set belt tension using the lift test, then
            check tracking and centre the belt if it drifts. Vacuum around and under the machine
            thoroughly, and if you are comfortable doing so and it is unplugged, vacuum the motor
            compartment, which on a machine that lived on carpet is frequently full of fibre.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is perhaps forty minutes of work and it materially changes the machine's remaining
            life. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>,
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt tension guide</a>
            and
            <a href="/how-to-center-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt centring guide</a>
            cover each step, and the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>
            sets the ongoing routine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-new-wins" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. When New Is Clearly the Right Answer</h2>
<p>
            Four situations where we would not recommend the used market, regardless of the value
            argument.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You are not confident assessing mechanical condition.</strong> The inspection
            is the entire risk control. If listening to a motor and judging whether a belt hesitates
            under load is not something you would trust yourself to do, buy new or refurbished.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You cannot arrange transport.</strong> A 250 lb machine you cannot collect is
            not a bargain.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Your budget is above about $2,000.</strong> New at that price is already good
            hardware with full warranty coverage on expensive components, and the hardware
            advantage of buying used narrows considerably.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You want a specific current model.</strong> The used market offers what exists,
            not what you chose. If you have decided on a particular machine, waiting for one to
            appear locally in good condition can take months.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Final Recommendations</h2>
<p>
            Under $900, buy used. At that budget new means a short deck, a small motor and a light
            frame, while used means a full-size machine that cost two or three times as much
            originally. The gap is large enough to be worth the inspection effort, and it is the
            only way to get a running deck at that money.
          </p>
          <p>
            Between $900 and $2,000, it genuinely depends on you. New is sufficient at this price
            and comes with recourse; used buys commercial-grade hardware. If you enjoy the process
            and can assess a machine, go used. If the idea is tiring, buy new and do not feel you
            have overpaid.
          </p>
          <p>
            Above $2,000, buy new — or buy manufacturer-refurbished, which is the best of both and
            worth watching for at any budget.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy secondhand, service it in week one. You are inheriting someone else's
            maintenance history, and forty minutes of work is the difference between a machine with
            years left and one that fails next winter.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth buying a used treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Under about $900, almost always. At that budget new buys a 40 to 55-inch deck and a light frame, while used buys a machine that originally cost two or three times as much, with a 60-inch deck and 250 lbs of frame. Above $2,000 the argument weakens, because new is already good hardware with full warranty cover.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should a used treadmill cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly half the original price at two years and about a third at three to five, then it flattens because the remaining value is essentially the frame. A machine that sold for $1,800 new should be around $600 at four years in average condition, less if it was never lubricated.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I check when buying a used treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Run it at several speeds and listen for a rumble that rises with speed. Stand on it and confirm the belt does not hesitate under load. Watch belt tracking for several minutes. Slide a hand under the belt — it should feel slightly slick, not dry or gritty. Check the deck for polishing, and test every console function including incline.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do treadmill warranties transfer to a second owner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost never. Manufacturer warranties are typically non-transferable, so assume zero coverage on any private sale regardless of how much time is nominally remaining. Refurbished and dealer purchases are the exception, usually carrying 90 days to a year, often parts-only.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the most common problem with used treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A motor and controller board worn by years of running against a dry, unlubricated deck. It is the expensive failure and it is nearly always caused by skipped maintenance rather than age. That is why the single most informative question to a seller is how often they lubricated it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a refurbished treadmill better than a used one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most buyers, yes. Wear parts are typically replaced, a limited warranty applies and delivery is usually handled, which removes the three main risks of private sale. You pay more than private sale and much less than new, and the constraint is availability — you buy what exists rather than what you chose.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Used Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The first two are the ones that turn a bargain into a repair bill, and both take five minutes to avoid.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying without running the machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A seller unwilling to plug it in and demonstrate it is telling you something. Run it at several speeds for a few minutes, stand on it, and listen for a rumble that rises with speed or a belt that hesitates under load. Everything expensive that can be wrong announces itself in that test.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking under the belt</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Slide a hand between belt and deck near the middle. Slightly slick is good. Dry, hot or gritty means it was never lubricated, and a machine that ran for years against a dry deck has a motor and controller that have already spent much of their life. This is the check that predicts the expensive failure.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the cost of moving it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A mid-range treadmill is 200 to 300 lbs and cannot be angled around a tight stairwell turn. Budget $80 to $200 for a van and plan on two capable people. Measure the route at both ends before agreeing a price — a $500 bargain you cannot get through your door is not a bargain.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not servicing it in the first week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">You are inheriting an unknown maintenance history, and the cheapest moment to correct it is immediately. Lubricate (after checking the manual for deck type), set belt tension, centre the belt and vacuum the motor compartment. Forty minutes of work materially changes how long the machine has left.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

